From: Charles Pellegrini <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:54:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH v3 1/6] lib: scatterlist: fix sg_calculate_split() nb_splits
 overshoot on partial coverage

sg_calculate_split() decrements nb_splits in two places per outer-loop
iteration: once in the inner while() that advances to the next output
split when a split boundary falls mid-entry, and once in the trailing

    if (!size && --nb_splits > 0)

The loop-termination check only tests for nb_splits == 0:

    if (!nb_splits)
        break;

When the final requested split ends mid-entry of an input entry that is
not the last one the pass iterates, both decrements run with their
bodies skipped: the inner while() takes nb_splits 1 -> 0, then the
trailing if() takes it 0 -> -1. The termination check sees -1, not 0, so
the loop does not break and every remaining input entry is appended to
the last split. Each appended entry gets len = min(0, sglen) = 0, so
length_last_sg ends up 0, while the entry in which the split should have
ended is no longer the last one and keeps its full length, as do the
appended entries other than the last. The split therefore covers more
bytes than requested: up to the end of the next-to-last input entry,
followed by a zero-length entry. With CONFIG_NEED_SG_DMA_LENGTH, for
in=[2, 1], skip=0, nb_splits=1, sizes=[1] the single output split is
{2, 0}: 2 bytes instead of 1; for in=[2, 1, 1] it is {2, 1, 0}. (Without
CONFIG_NEED_SG_DMA_LENGTH the ->length clobber fixed by the next patch
also zeroes the non-last entries, and the first example gives {0, 0}.)

The mapped pass (in_mapped_nents != 0) runs the same function over the
DMA segmentation, which can be coarser than the CPU one. There the DMA
pass can overshoot while the CPU pass does not, so it counts more
entries for a split than sg_split() allocated from the CPU pass, and
sg_split_mapped() writes past the end of that split's output array. For
example, with three physically contiguous 5-byte entries mapped as two
DMA segments [10, 5] and sizes=[5], the CPU pass ends on the edge of
entry 0 (one entry allocated) while the DMA pass ends mid-segment 0 and
overshoots to two entries: sg_split_mapped() writes one entry past the
allocation (a heap-buffer-overflow write in a userspace ASan build).

Widen the termination check to also catch the overshoot to -1:

    if (nb_splits <= 0)
        break;

The new condition only fires when nb_splits is already negative, which is
reachable solely through the double decrement above, so it cannot change
behaviour for any input that splits correctly today.

This was first reported by Alexander Egorenkov in 2021, with an identical
root-cause analysis; that patch was never reviewed or merged. Its proposed
fix moved the existing termination check above the second decrement rather
than widening it. That handles the mid-entry case but regresses
edge-aligned partial coverage -- a split ending exactly on an input-entry
boundary with further entries trailing -- which the current code handles
correctly. A KUnit test added later in this series
(sg_split_t10_edge_first_two_trailing) covers that regression.

No in-tree caller triggers the bug today: four request full coverage or
re-split over a DMA-mapped subset, and the one caller that requests
partial coverage (the DTHEv2 AEAD driver) bounds its consumer by cryptlen,
masking the wrong geometry. The fix matters for callers that request
partial coverage, which the kernel-doc allows: it defines out[k] as the
byte range starting at skip + sizes[0] + ... + sizes[k - 1], which only
requires the requested ranges to fit in the input, not to cover it.

Fixes: f8bcbe62acd0 ("lib: scatterlist: add sg splitting function")
Reported-by: Alexander Egorenkov <egorenar-dev@posteo.net>
Closes: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20210418143425.22944-1-egorenar-dev@posteo.net/
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-4-8 hegel-c
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5 hegel-c
---
 lib/sg_split.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/sg_split.c b/lib/sg_split.c
index 24e8f5e48..28fe7fa6c 100644
--- a/lib/sg_split.c
+++ b/lib/sg_split.c
@@ -67,7 +67,7 @@ static int sg_calculate_split(struct scatterlist *in, int nents, int nb_splits,
 			size = *(++sizes);
 		}
 
-		if (!nb_splits)
+		if (nb_splits <= 0)
 			break;
 	}
 
